<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shen Dao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 350–275 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shen Dao was a Chinese philosopher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, a native of Zhao who taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jixia-academy">Jixia Academy</a> in Qi. He stressed shi, the power that comes from position rather than personal virtue, and the impartial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>. His ideas were taken up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a> and became part of the tradition later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a>; only fragments of his writings survive.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the fourth century BCE the Tian rulers supported scholars at the Jixia Academy near the city's Ji Gate with stipends and houses; Mencius, Xunzi, Zou Yan, <b>Shen Dao</b> and Tian Pian were among those associated with it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Their ideas shaped Shen Buhai, chancellor of the Han state; <b>Shen Dao</b>, who argued that the realm be modelled on the natural world; and Han Fei, whom many consider the greatest Legalist thinker.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
